# Authoring a compose-plugins plug-in: an agent's guide to contract 0.3 [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]

> A plug-in is a Python package with a `manifest.json` and one `ComposePlugin` subclass. Write the manifest to the frozen field set, declare every bus namespace, build the manifest from the class, check it locally against a stub host, and never answer a real host's consent screens yourself.

---

## 1. Orientation

### [CXC-PA-WHAT] A plug-in is a package with a static manifest and one ComposePlugin subclass that a host installs after the operator consents

- claim: the manifest is read before any plug-in code runs and is authoritative for consent; the class is authoritative for runtime ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]). The bus is available, never required ([CXC-PC-RULE-BUS-OPTIONAL]).
- rule: the `contract` value you write is "0.3" when you use a 0.3 field and the host implements 0.3, otherwise "0.2"; a host refuses a newer MINOR naming both versions ([CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3], [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE]). Contract 0.3 is ratified but not yet built: write "0.2" until a host reports "0.3". A host reports its contract as the class attribute `Host.contract` (default `compose_plugins.version.HOST_CONTRACT`, "0.2" in compose-plugins 0.2.0); for your own stub host and for the library you build against, read that constant.
- rule: find this guide's nodes with the id prefix `CXC-PA-` (keep the trailing hyphen; `CXC-PA` alone also matches unrelated ids).
- read-at: python/compose_plugins/ in composeio main at 058d40c9ca2c4ae344c0546a3012580fef750f72 (compose-plugins 0.2.0).
- evidence: one plug-in (a periodic STATE publisher) was built cold from revision 1 of this guide and passed at both isolation levels; it needed 7 looks outside the guide. Revision 2 was re-tested the same way on 2026-11-28: a second periodic STATE publisher was built cold from the guide alone with 0 looks outside it, and passed all four acceptance criteria at both isolation levels. Revision 3 makes seven small corrections from that test's notes, each checked against the library source; it was not re-run.
- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LAYERS], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE], [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE], [CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3]

### [CXC-PA-SETUP] Library setup without installing it needs two PYTHONPATH entries, pydantic, and `python -m`

- rule: in a composeio checkout, put both `python/compose_plugins` and `python/composebus` on `PYTHONPATH` (joined with `:`); then `import compose_plugins` and `import composebus` work with nothing installed.
- rule: the one outside dependency is `pydantic` 2 or later (`pip install pydantic`; the repository has no copy). Python 3.10 or later.
- rule: the `compose-plugin` console script exists only after `pip install ./python/composebus ./python/compose_plugins`. Without an install run the same commands as modules: `python -m compose_plugins.derive build <package_dir>` and `python -m compose_plugins.conformance ...`.
- rule: keep your own package directory off `PYTHONPATH` for the build and for any host run, because an install must load from the installed copy; put it on `PYTHONPATH` ONLY for a direct class test ([CXC-PA-PLUGIN-CLASS]).
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIBRARY], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]

---

## 2. The frozen manifest

### [CXC-PA-MANIFEST-FIELDS] The frozen manifest at 0.3: every field, its type, an example and its owner node

- claim: the field set is frozen ([CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN]); an unknown key and every Home Assistant name is refused ([CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]). Required first, then optional; `config_schema` and `bus` are generated by the build. Every field is valid under any `contract` from 0.1 except `license` and `eula` (from 0.2) and `capabilities` and `warnings` (from 0.3). Omit every optional field you do not use; do not write empty values. With no real documentation page yet, use the placeholder `https://example.org/<domain>`.

| field | requiredness and type | example | owner |
|---|---|---|---|
| `contract` | required, string; write "0.2" until the host reports it supports "0.3" (the released library, 0.2.0, refuses "0.3") | "0.2" | CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3 |
| `domain` | required, string; your bus root is plugins/<domain>/ | "hello_world" | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `name` | required, string | "Hello World" | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `version` | required, SemVer string | "1.0.0" | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `description` | required, one-line string | "Greets on the bus." | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `documentation` | required, https URL | "https://example.org/hello_world" | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `requirements` | required, list; MUST be [] today | [] | CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL |
| `run` | required, object | {"form": "python", "entry": "hello_world.plugin:HelloWorld"} | CXC-PC-RUN |
| `hosts` | required, object, one key or more | {"composeio": {}, "gnomon": {}} | CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS |
| `issue_tracker` | optional, URL | "https://example.org/hello_world/issues" | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `codeowners` | optional, list of handles | ["@example-org"] | CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS |
| `isolation_floor` | optional, "inprocess" (default) or "subprocess" | "inprocess" | CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE |
| `bus` | optional, object; generated | see CXC-PA-BUS-DECLARATIONS | CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK |
| `standard_questions` | optional, relative directory | "standard_questions" | CXC-PC-STANDARD-QUESTIONS |
| `permissions` | optional, object `{network, filesystem}` | {"network": [{"direction": "outbound", "target": "printer.local:631", "purpose": "send print jobs"}]} | CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS |
| `license` | optional, SPDX string, from 0.2 | "MIT" | CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD |
| `eula` | optional, package-relative file, from 0.2 | "EULA.txt" | CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD |
| `capabilities` | optional, list of tokens, from 0.3 | ["agent-messaging"] | CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES |
| `warnings` | optional, list of `{id, title, text}`, from 0.3 | [{"id": "sends-messages", "title": "Sends messages", "text": "Sends messages to agents."}] | CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS |
| `config_schema` | generated, object (JSON Schema) | {"type": "object", "properties": {"greeting": {"type": "string", "default": "hello"}}} | CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE |

- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN], [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES], [CXC-PC-RUN], [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK], [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS], [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]

### [CXC-PA-RUN-ISOLATION-HOSTS] `run` is python only, the isolation floor is raised and never forced down, and `hosts` lists the hosts you support

- rule: `run.form` is `python` and `run.entry` is `module:Class` naming a `ComposePlugin` subclass; your top-level import package must not shadow `composebus`, `compose_plugins`, `composeio` or a standard-library module ([CXC-PC-RUN]).
- rule: `isolation_floor` is `inprocess` or `subprocess`; a host runs you at that level or higher, so write nothing that works only inside the host process ([CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE]).
- rule: a `hosts` key means that host is supported; ComposeIO's section is `{}`, and Gnomon's section content is Gnomon's own, so read its spec before declaring `gnomon` in a shipped plug-in ([CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT] `host-section-hook`).
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-RUN], [CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE], [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT]

---

## 3. Bus, config and declared claims

### [CXC-PA-BUS-DECLARATIONS] Every namespace is declared as STATE or EVENT under `plugins/<domain>/` on the class, and the build writes the `bus` block

- rule: put `publishes`, `subscribes` and `bus_types` (namespace to "STATE" or "EVENT") on the class; `bus_types` covers exactly the namespaces in the two lists ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE] `bus-types-attribute`). The type and namespace rules are [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK] and [CXC-PC-RULE-REAL-TYPES].
- example: {"publishes": [{"namespace": "plugins/hello_world/greeting", "type": "STATE"}, {"namespace": "plugins/hello_world/pong", "type": "EVENT"}], "subscribes": [{"namespace": "plugins/hello_world/ping", "type": "EVENT"}]}
- boundary-case: the build fails naming the namespace when one has no type or `bus_types` names one that is in neither list.
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK], [CXC-PC-RULE-REAL-TYPES], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]

### [CXC-PA-CONFIG] Config is one pydantic `Config` model with defaults; keep grant-bearing names out of it

- rule: nest `class Config(BaseModel)` in the plug-in class and give every field a default; use `str`, `int`, `float`, `bool` or an enum; pydantic constraints such as `Field(ge=1)` are allowed and appear in the schema (as `minimum`). The build writes its JSON Schema as `config_schema`, and a plug-in with no `Config` gets the empty-object schema ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]). Invalid stored config parks the plug-in before it is built ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-CONFIG]).
- rule: a field whose name or format names a bus namespace, a host, a path or a grant draws a named advisory, "this config field looks grant-bearing", under contract "0.3". It is a structural author aid, not enforcement; declare reach in `bus` and `permissions` instead ([CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG]).
- example: `class Config(BaseModel): greeting: str = "hello"`.
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-CONFIG], [CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG], [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS]

### [CXC-PA-DECLARED-CLAIMS] `license`, `eula`, `capabilities` and `warnings` are optional claims shown at consent and never verified

- rule: `eula` is shown in full at consent and a changed EULA asks again ([CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT]); `license` is informational ([CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD]).
- rule: declare `agent-messaging` in `capabilities`, and say it in a `warnings` entry, if you send to or receive from agents ([CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS]). Adding a token, or adding or changing a warning's text, in an upgrade makes the operator consent again ([CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE]).
- example: {"capabilities": ["agent-messaging"], "warnings": [{"id": "sends-messages", "title": "Sends messages", "text": "Sends messages to agents."}]}
- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT], [CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD], [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS], [CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE]

---

## 4. The plug-in class

### [CXC-PA-PLUGIN-CLASS] The entry point is one ComposePlugin subclass with a Config, an identity, the declared namespaces and bus_types

- rule: subclass `composebus.plugin.ComposePlugin`; define `Config`, `name` (equal to `domain`), `version` (equal to the manifest `version`), `publishes`, `subscribes` and `bus_types: ClassVar[dict]`. The host builds an instance for you; in a test build one with `Cls(Cls.Config())` or `Cls({"field": value})` (this direct class test needs the package directory on `PYTHONPATH`, and only for that test; [CXC-PA-SETUP]), and read config as `self.config.<field>`. Import only `composebus`, `pydantic` and the standard library; never `composeio` or a host ([CXC-PC-RULE-BUS-OPTIONAL], [CXC-PC-LIBRARY]).
- example: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/fixtures/hello_world_v1/hello_world/plugin.py lines 20 to 28 declare `class HelloWorld(ComposePlugin)` with `name = "hello_world"`, `publishes = [GREETING, PONG]`, `subscribes = [PING]` and `bus_types = {GREETING: "STATE", PONG: "EVENT", PING: "EVENT"}`.
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE], [CXC-PC-LIBRARY], [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1]

### [CXC-PA-CALLBACKS] The callbacks are `start(bus)`, `stop()` and the optional `health_reason()`; traffic goes through four bus calls

- rule: `start(self, bus)` attaches to the bus and begins; `stop(self)` releases everything and MUST be safe to call twice (the bus has no unsubscribe, so guard handlers with a running flag); `health_reason(self)` returns a short reason string while unwell, else None, and the host then reports `degraded(<reason>)` ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-HEALTH]).
- rule: STATE: `bus.publish_state(namespace, value, source="plugin:<domain>")`. EVENT: `bus.publish_event(namespace, event_type, data_dict, source="plugin:<domain>")`. Subscribe: `bus.subscribe_events(namespace, callback)` with callback `(namespace, event_type, data)`. To read a STATE back (for example on the stub host), call `bus.get_state(namespace)`, which returns the current value, and None for a tombstoned or unknown namespace; or `bus.subscribe(namespace, callback)` with callback `(namespace, new, old)`. Use only namespaces you declared; your retained STATE topics are tombstoned on uninstall ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UNINSTALL]).
- rule: a plug-in may run its own timer thread for periodic publishing. Make it a daemon that waits on a `threading.Event`; `stop()` MUST set the event and join the thread, so nothing is published after `stop()` returns, and `health_reason()` may report a dead thread.
- example: hello_world_v1/hello_world/plugin.py lines 30 to 35 (`start`), 40 to 47 (`_on_ping`), 49 to 54 (`stop`, `health_reason`).
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-HEALTH], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UNINSTALL], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1], [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK]

---

## 5. Worked example, packaging and verification

### [CXC-PA-WORKED-EXAMPLE] hello_world_eula is the reference: copy its layout and class shape

- claim: the reference is the contract-0.2 fixture python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/fixtures/hello_world_eula/ in the composeio repository ([CXC-PC-HELLO-V1]): manifest.json (`"contract": "0.2"`, `"eula": "EULA.txt"`), EULA.txt beside it, and hello_world/__init__.py and hello_world/plugin.py (the import package and the class `HelloWorld`; line numbers cited in this guide match plugin.py). A root `__init__.py` is optional: the fixture has one at its package root, empty and unused (the installer copies the tree and the loader puts the package root on `sys.path`; neither reads it).
- rule: copy the layout, rename `hello_world`, then rebuild the manifest ([CXC-PA-PACKAGING]). If you have no EULA, delete the `eula` key and `EULA.txt`. Do not copy its `codeowners` or its example.invalid URLs.
- rule: older fixtures differ: hello_world_v1/ is contract "0.1" (change it to "0.2" when you copy it), and hello_world_v2/ adds config `repeat: int = 1` and a subscribe that widens the grant, so an upgrade asks consent again ([CXC-PC-HELLO-V2]). Their plugin.py files declare the same class shape.
- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V2], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD], [CXC-PA-PACKAGING]

### [CXC-PA-PACKAGING] Packaging is a source folder or a wheel with `manifest.json` at the package root, built with `compose-plugin build`

- rule: ship a local directory or a wheel; git URLs and indexes are refused ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL]). A host reads `manifest.json` without importing anything: at the directory root, or as package data at `<import-package>/manifest.json` in a wheel with one such package ([CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FILE], [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (9)).
- rule: hand-write the core fields, then run `compose-plugin build <package_dir>` (without an install: `python -m compose_plugins.derive build <package_dir>`, [CXC-PA-SETUP]); it imports the class in a child process and rewrites `manifest.json` with `config_schema` and `bus`. Rebuild after any change to `Config`, `publishes`, `subscribes` or `bus_types`; a class and manifest that disagree refuse the enable ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]).
- rule: the installer records the sha256 of the exact `manifest.json` bytes, so ship the built bytes and never edit them afterwards. The build imports your class and leaves `__pycache__` directories in the package folder: keep them out by setting `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` for the build and for every test run (preferred, no deletion needed); if any exist, delete every `__pycache__` under the package folder before you package it ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL] `manifest-sha256-definition`).
- verify-machine: the build exits 0 and prints "compose-plugin build: wrote <path>/manifest.json".
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FILE], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL]

### [CXC-PA-VERIFY-LOCAL] Local verification runs against a stub host only: read the manifest, validate it, and drive a PluginManager

- rule: setup first ([CXC-PA-SETUP]). Step 1, read without executing: `from compose_plugins.package import read_manifest; mf = read_manifest("<package_dir_or_wheel>")`. Step 2, validate: `from compose_plugins.manifest import validate_manifest; validate_manifest(mf.data, host=NAME, section_validator=lambda section: [], package=mf.package)`; a refusal raises `ManifestRefused` naming every field.
- rule: Stub host. Subclass `compose_plugins.host.Host` in your own test process: `super().__init__(NAME, state_root=<fresh empty directory>, minimum_isolation="inprocess")` (or "subprocess"); implement `consent_gate(request)` returning `ConsentDecision("granted", "<operator name>")` (import `ConsentDecision` from `compose_plugins.host`), `validate_section(section)` and `render_section(section)` returning `[]`, and `bus()` returning ONE `composebus.bus.ComposeIOBus(bus_id="test")` that you build once in the stub's `__init__` and keep on the instance; `bus()` returns that same object on every call and never builds a bus (a new bus per call reads empty state).
- rule: NAME MUST be a key in your manifest's `hosts` (name the stub `composeio` and your shipped manifest validates unchanged). Never edit `manifest.json` to fit a test host; tests/wave2_helpers.py does edit it (`manifest_for_testhost`), which is a test helper, not a pattern to copy.
- rule: Step 3, drive the lifecycle: `from compose_plugins.lifecycle import PluginManager; pm = PluginManager(host)`; `p = pm.prepare_install(src)`; `pm.install(src, consented_sha256=p.manifest_sha256, operator="op", accepted=True)`; `pm.configure(domain, {"field": value})` (a plain dict); `pm.enable(domain)` and `pm.disable(domain)` (each returns the status); `pm.uninstall(domain)` (returns the uninstalled record, a dict with keys `domain`, `event_seq`, `last_version`, `state`, `uninstalled_at`); `pm.tick()` runs a status tick; `pm.close()` at the end. Read output with `host.bus().get_state(namespace)` or a subscription ([CXC-PA-CALLBACKS]).
- rule: Names. `pm.state(domain)` is one of `available`, `installed`, `enabled`, `disabled`, `uninstalled`; `pm.status(domain)` is `running`, `stopped`, `parked(<reason>)`, `degraded(<reason>)` or `failed(<reason>)`. A call from a state that does not allow it raises `TransitionRefused` (the table `ALLOWED` in lifecycle.py): configure from installed, enabled or disabled; enable from installed or disabled; disable from enabled; uninstall from installed, enabled or disabled.
- rule: `configure` on an ENABLED plug-in only stores the config; it applies at the next `enable`, so disable then enable to see it take effect.
- rule: these steps run ONLY against that stub host, in your own process. Never run step 3 against, or send any request to, a real host's Plugin Manager ([CXC-PA-NO-CONSENT-BY-AGENT]).
- example: python/compose_plugins/tests/wave2_helpers.py defines `TestHost(Host)` and `install(pm, src)`; copy the host class and the install call only, and do not copy `manifest_for_testhost` or `fixture_copy`, which edit `manifest.json`.
- verify-machine: step 2 returns a `Manifest` without raising. Step 3 passes when: after `enable` the status is `running` and the output you expect is read back; after `disable` the state is `disabled` and nothing more is published; after `uninstall` the state is `uninstalled`, the installed code directory `<state_root>/installed/<domain>/` is gone, a retained STATE reads None, and (in process) no module of your package remains in `sys.modules`. Run it at both isolation levels if your host supports both.
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FILE], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-STATES], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES], [CXC-PA-SETUP], [CXC-PA-NO-CONSENT-BY-AGENT]

### [CXC-PA-VERIFY-CONFORMANCE] The conformance script tests a HOST with the hello-world fixtures, not your plug-in; it passes when it exits 0

- claim: `python -m compose_plugins.conformance --target <module>:<factory> --isolation inprocess|subprocess` runs the nine-step lifecycle and the negative controls against a host built by `factory(isolation, workdir)`. It takes no plug-in of yours ([CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT]).
- rule: "passes" is exit status 0 with every step and control `PASS` and the summary line `SUMMARY host=<name> isolation=<level> contract=<version>: <n> passed, 0 failed of <n>`; exit 1 is a failure and exit 2 a refusal before step 1. Run it only when you build or change a host, and never edit the shared fixtures to make it pass.
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT], [CXC-PC-CONTRACT-AUTHORITATIVE], [CXC-PC-NEGATIVE-CONTROLS]

---

## 6. What an agent never does, and the checklist

### [CXC-PA-NO-CONSENT-BY-AGENT] An agent NEVER drives a host's state-changing route (consent, warning acknowledgement, enable, disable, configure, uninstall and its confirm); the operator does

- rule: consent belongs to the host and to its operator, never to the plug-in or to an agent working on it ([CXC-PC-RULE-CONSENT-HOST-OWNED], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES]). Do not press Accept or Refuse, set an acknowledgement box, or POST `decision=accept`, `decision=confirm` or any decision value to a real host's install or upgrade routes; and do not enable, disable, configure or uninstall a plug-in on a real host through its routes either ([CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK], [CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM], [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS]). Read-only pages (index, detail, status) are fine to read.
- rule: the host's wall behind this rule is [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-AGENT-BLOCK]: a host MUST keep these routes out of agents' reach. Do not rely on the wall; keep out anyway.
- rule: when a real install, upgrade or uninstall is needed, hand the operator the package path and what to look for; the operator reads the page and decides. A host's auth hook is the operator's, not yours.
- rule: local verification uses only the stub host of [CXC-PA-VERIFY-LOCAL], where the gate is yours by construction.
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-RULE-CONSENT-HOST-OWNED], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES], [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-AGENT-BLOCK], [CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK], [CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM], [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS]

### [CXC-PA-CHECKLIST] The DO and DON'T list an agent checks before shipping

- rule: DO state a `contract` at or below the host's and write every required field ([CXC-PA-MANIFEST-FIELDS]).
- rule: DO declare every namespace you use, each STATE or EVENT under `plugins/<domain>/`, with `bus_types` matching ([CXC-PA-BUS-DECLARATIONS]).
- rule: DO give every `Config` field a default, run `compose-plugin build` after every class change, and ship the built bytes ([CXC-PA-CONFIG], [CXC-PA-PACKAGING]).
- rule: DO make `stop` safe to call twice, and end and join any thread you started ([CXC-PA-CALLBACKS]).
- rule: DO omit optional fields you do not use, and DO name your stub host a key of `hosts` ([CXC-PA-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PA-VERIFY-LOCAL]).
- rule: DO declare the network and filesystem access you need in `permissions`, and `agent-messaging` with a warning if you message agents ([CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS], [CXC-PA-DECLARED-CLAIMS]).
- rule: DON'T put a bus namespace, host, URL, path or permission in config ([CXC-PA-CONFIG]).
- rule: DON'T use the network or the filesystem beyond what you declared; a declaration is shown to the operator and never checked, so undeclared use misleads the person who consented ([CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS] `never-verified-scope`).
- rule: DON'T import `composeio` or any host module; DON'T name your import package after `composebus`, `compose_plugins`, `composeio` or a standard-library module ([CXC-PA-RUN-ISOLATION-HOSTS], [CXC-PA-PLUGIN-CLASS]).
- rule: DON'T list `requirements` (it MUST be `[]`), add a key the contract does not define, use a reserved Home Assistant name, edit `manifest.json` after building (not even to fit a test host), or ship `__pycache__` ([CXC-PA-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PA-PACKAGING]).
- rule: DON'T drive any state-changing route of a real host: consent, acknowledgement, enable, disable, configure, uninstall or its confirm ([CXC-PA-NO-CONSENT-BY-AGENT]).
- rule: when a refusal names a field, fix that field's node above and rebuild; a refusal naming a version means lower `contract` or use a host that implements it ([CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE]).
- basis: contract-owner design call
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-AUTHORING]
- see-also: [CXC-PA-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PA-BUS-DECLARATIONS], [CXC-PA-CONFIG], [CXC-PA-PACKAGING], [CXC-PA-CALLBACKS], [CXC-PA-DECLARED-CLAIMS], [CXC-PA-NO-CONSENT-BY-AGENT], [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS]

